    { "type": "paragraph", "html": "For most of the last 23 million years, Europe was never a solid wall of trees. Mammoths, rhinos, giant deer, aurochs and wild horses kept the landscape open — grazing, trampling, and browsing a shifting mosaic of woodland and grassland into being." },
    { "type": "paragraph", "html": "<strong>That changed with the arrival of modern humans.</strong> Between roughly 40,000 and 15,000 years ago, nearly every large herbivore over 300kg was pushed toward extinction or retreat. By the time the LGM's ice began to melt, the animals that had structured the landscape for millennia were already gone or going." },
